codex-luna is the interesting case and it is NOT the thin suite its count suggests. Its four
tests are table-driven: one loops over all nine historical matrices, one asserts the genuine
partition AND its one-cell mutation together with the comment "either assertion alone admits a
constant implementation", one covers five boundaries in a single body. It covers the same
ground in 111 lines that gemini takes 317 to cover. Test-function count is not coverage, and
this project uses it as a proxy in three places. (farmerbob-aqg2)

gemini-38-flash: correct and thorough, and codex's critique lands the one real observation --
it duplicates the nine historical matrices across two test bodies. Redundant rather than
defective, but it is the same rule in two places, on the task about the same rule in two places.

